{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"dcdb2e670ae8b3d4","name":"card_event_second_front","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/Germany/Homefront/events/card_event_second_front","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"SECOND FRONT","text":"Remove a German ground unit. Return it at the end of the next enemy turn, then duplicate it.","flavorText":"The Allied landings in Normandy on 6 June 1944 opened a second front in Europe, posing a strategic nightmare for Germany. The Wehrmacht now had to fight on two major fronts against numerically and logistically superior enemies. Hitler insisted on holding the Atlantic Wall at all costs, ordering divisions like the 19th Panzer Division to stand their ground rather than maneuver. German commanders such as Rommel and Rundstedt disagreed on tactics, but both faced severe limitations due to Allied air superiority and fuel shortages. In response to the landings, Germany rushed reinforcements from other theaters. Units were transferred from Italy and Scandinavia, and dozens of divisions were pulled from the Eastern Front, weakening defenses against the advancing Red Army. In the weeks following D-Day, Soviet forces launched Operation Bagration, one of the largest offensives of the war. German lines in the East collapsed, losing hundreds of thousands of men and exposing Poland and the Baltics. The opening of the Western Front did not just split Germany’s forces; it accelerated the downfall of Germany’s empire on both sides of the continent.","faction":"Germany","type":"order","rarity":"Common","cost":4,"operationCost":0,"attack":0,"defense":0},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_event_second_front","title":{"Namespace":"card_germany","Key":"card_event_second_front_title","SourceString":"SECOND FRONT","LocalizedString":"SECOND FRONT"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::order","faction":"EFactionEnum::Germany","kredits":4,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_germany","Key":"card_event_second_front_text","SourceString":"Remove a German ground unit. Return it at the end of the next enemy turn, then duplicate it.","LocalizedString":"Remove a German ground unit. Return it at the end of the next enemy turn, then duplicate it."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_germany","Key":"card_event_second_front_history","SourceString":"The Allied landings in Normandy on 6 June 1944 opened a second front in Europe, posing a strategic nightmare for Germany. The Wehrmacht now had to fight on two major fronts against numerically and logistically superior enemies. Hitler insisted on holding the Atlantic Wall at all costs, ordering divisions like the 19th Panzer Division to stand their ground rather than maneuver. German commanders such as Rommel and Rundstedt disagreed on tactics, but both faced severe limitations due to Allied air superiority and fuel shortages. In response to the landings, Germany rushed reinforcements from other theaters. Units were transferred from Italy and Scandinavia, and dozens of divisions were pulled from the Eastern Front, weakening defenses against the advancing Red Army. In the weeks following D-Day, Soviet forces launched Operation Bagration, one of the largest offensives of the war. German lines in the East collapsed, losing hundreds of thousands of men and exposing Poland and the Baltics. The opening of the Western Front did not just split Germany’s forces; it accelerated the downfall of Germany’s empire on both sides of the continent.","LocalizedString":"The Allied landings in Normandy on 6 June 1944 opened a second front in Europe, posing a strategic nightmare for Germany. The Wehrmacht now had to fight on two major fronts against numerically and logistically superior enemies. Hitler insisted on holding the Atlantic Wall at all costs, ordering divisions like the 19th Panzer Division to stand their ground rather than maneuver. German commanders such as Rommel and Rundstedt disagreed on tactics, but both faced severe limitations due to Allied air superiority and fuel shortages. In respons